83
00:10:17.230 --> 00:10:29.919
Kevin Millikin: we describe the essentials of the programming model. It's based on some research that was a model called Kachina, which was a very abstract model of how a 0 knowledge, smart contract system would work

84
00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:41.079
Kevin Millikin: and compact is a specific realization of the Kachina model. So the core ingredients are that the contracts are run off chain

85
00:10:41.330 --> 00:10:43.759
Kevin Millikin: with full access to private data.

86
00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:56.469
Kevin Millikin: This is any arbitrary code we use typescript, but it could be compiled to rust code, or it could be compiled to python or machine language, or your custom bespoke whatever

87
00:10:57.126 --> 00:11:05.449
Kevin Millikin: there is also on-chain code that implements only the contract's public state updates and has no access to private data.

88
00:11:05.770 --> 00:11:14.750
Kevin Millikin: And then the key that unlocks the fact that it's safe for the chain to run the on chain code is that the transaction also contains a 0 knowledge proof

89
00:11:14.930 --> 00:11:22.179
Kevin Millikin: that the off-chain execution was correct, and the chain will verify the Zk proof.

90
00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:28.659
Kevin Millikin: and then, if it can verify it, it's it's okay to run the on-chain code.

91
00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:34.249
Kevin Millikin: So our in the midnight network we compile to Javascript code.

92
00:11:34.320 --> 00:12:00.930
Kevin Millikin: which is the off chain execution. The on-chain execution is a custom virtual machine that we've designed for public state updates. The proof is a implementation of the Planck proving system that we've built, and then the blockchain verifies the proofs and executes the public part of the contract. One important piece here that is not really part of compact is part of the midnight blockchain is that

93
00:12:01.190 --> 00:12:20.100
Kevin Millikin: the public state updates have to be coherent with what was performed off chain, and a transaction can fail. If, for instance, you read a value from the public state, and it's different. So part of the value add of the midnight blockchain is that we do a lot to minimize the possibility of transaction failures for that reason.

